var e=[{slug:`break-even-multiple-products-sales-mix`,tag:`Break-Even Concepts`,name:`Break-Even Concepts`,cluster:`concepts`,title:`Break-Even for Multiple Products: The Sales Mix Method`,seoTitle:`Break-Even for Multiple Products: Sales Mix`,description:`Calculate break-even for multiple products using contribution margins and a realistic sales mix. See a checked example and learn when the answer changes.`,metaDescription:`Calculate break-even for multiple products using contribution margins and a realistic sales mix. See a checked example and learn when the answer changes.`,image:`/images/blog/multi-product-break-even-editorial.webp`,alt:`Small shop owner comparing three products and a sales worksheet at a worktable`,imageCaption:`Illustrative editorial image; all figures below are fictional planning assumptions.`,published:`2026-09-29`,modified:`2026-09-29`,opening:`Your shop sells mugs, tote bags and candles. A mug might leave $12 after its direct costs, while a tote leaves $20. Dividing monthly rent by the margin on a mug will give the wrong answer for the whole shop. You need to know what proportion of each item you expect to sell.`,unit:`sales`,singular:`sale`,calculatorSlug:`small-business-break-even-calculator`,tags:[`Break-Even Concepts`,`Sales Mix`,`Small Business`],features:[`A checked worked example`,`Clearly stated assumptions`,`Practical capacity and risk checks`],html:`<p>Your shop sells mugs, tote bags and candles. A mug might leave $12 after its direct costs, while a tote leaves $20. Dividing monthly rent by the margin on a mug will give the wrong answer for the whole shop. You need to know <strong>what proportion of each item you expect to sell</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Subtract each product's variable cost from its selling price. Multiply each contribution margin by that product's share of expected unit sales, then add the results. Divide monthly fixed costs by this weighted average contribution margin. Round the resulting <em>total units</em> up and check whether the implied product quantities and operating capacity are realistic. Recalculate if your mix changes.</p>
<p>This is a planning model, not a prediction that customers will buy in exactly that ratio. Here is a complete example you can audit and adapt.</p>
<h2 id="start-with-the-margin-each-sale-actually-contributes">Start with the margin each sale actually contributes</h2>
<p>Contribution margin is selling price minus the cost that moves with that sale. Imagine a small maker with these <strong>illustrative</strong> prices and costs in USD. The figures are examples, not market averages.</p>
<div class="article-table-scroll"><table>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Product</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Selling price</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Variable cost per sale</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Contribution per sale</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Expected unit mix</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Mug</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$30</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$18</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$12</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">50%</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Tote</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$40</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$20</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$20</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">30%</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Candle</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$25</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$15</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$10</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">20%</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The maker expects 100 sales to look roughly like 50 mugs, 30 totes and 20 candles. This ratio is <strong>5:3:2</strong>. It is based on an example forecast; your own mix should come from recent orders, a credible preorder book or a clearly labeled launch assumption.</p>
<p>The $18 variable cost for a mug might include materials, packaging, payment processing, per-order fulfillment and a sales commission. Use the same cost boundary for all three products. Monthly rent, software and a planned owner salary belong with fixed expenses if they do not change with each extra item. If a marketplace charges a percentage of the sale, put that charge with the relevant product's variable costs. Read our <a href="/blogs/calculate-contribution-margin/">contribution margin guide</a> if you need help separating these items.</p>
<h2 id="calculate-one-weighted-margin-for-the-whole-basket">Calculate one weighted margin for the whole basket</h2>
<p>Multiply each item's contribution by its share of unit sales:</p>
<ul>
<li>Mugs: $12 × 50% = <strong>$6</strong>.</li>
<li>Totes: $20 × 30% = <strong>$6</strong>.</li>
<li>Candles: $10 × 20% = <strong>$2</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Together, the weighted average contribution is <strong>$14 per item sold</strong>. In plain English, an average sale under this mix contributes $14 toward shared monthly expenses. You have not blended the prices, and you have not pretended every product earns $14 individually.</p>
<p>Assume fixed monthly expenses of $7,000, including the owner's planned pay. Business-wide break-even is <strong>$7,000 ÷ $14 = 500 items per month</strong>. At the planned 5:3:2 mix, that means 250 mugs, 150 totes and 100 candles.</p>
<p>Check the result from the product level: 250 × $12 + 150 × $20 + 100 × $10 = <strong>$7,000 contribution</strong>. After $7,000 of fixed costs, modeled operating profit is zero. The 500-unit threshold depends on maintaining the stated mix and prices. It does not tell you that selling any arbitrary collection of 500 products will work. OpenStax's <a href="https://openstax.org/books/principles-managerial-accounting/pages/3-4-perform-break-even-sensitivity-analysis-for-a-multi-product-environment-under-changing-business-situations">multi-product break-even lesson</a> uses the same composite-unit logic and explains why a changed sales mix changes the answer.</p>
<h3 id="the-alternative-calculate-a-composite-basket">The alternative: calculate a composite basket</h3>
<p>If percentages feel abstract, group a representative 10 sales: five mugs, three totes and two candles. Their combined contribution is (5 × $12) + (3 × $20) + (2 × $10) = <strong>$140 per ten-item basket</strong>. The shop needs $7,000 ÷ $140 = <strong>50 such baskets</strong>, or 500 individual items. This is the same calculation in a format some teams find easier to discuss during inventory planning.</p>
<p>Do not automatically sell a ten-item bundle to a customer: the basket is an accounting shorthand for expected standalone sales. If the real ratio is awkward, calculate weighted margin using percentages first, then round item targets sensibly and recheck the dollars.</p>
<h2 id="what-if-customers-buy-more-of-the-lower-margin-item">What if customers buy more of the lower-margin item?</h2>
<p>Here is the decision that makes the method useful. Say the shop is still forecasting 500 sales, but a promotion shifts the mix to <strong>70% mugs, 10% totes, 20% candles</strong>. The new weighted contribution is $12 × 70% + $20 × 10% + $10 × 20% = <strong>$12.40 per sale</strong>. Five hundred sales now contribute <strong>$6,200</strong>, leaving an <strong>$800 shortfall</strong> against fixed costs. The order count looks healthy; the margin mix does not.</p>
<p>At the new mix, $7,000 ÷ $12.40 = 564.52, so plan for <strong>at least 565 individual sales</strong> as a unit-level approximation. At exactly 565 sales, the percentages imply fractional items; choose whole quantities and verify contribution. For example, 396 mugs, 56 totes and 113 candles total 565 items and contribute $4,752 + $1,120 + $1,130 = <strong>$7,002</strong>. That is only $2 over break-even. The particular whole-item combination is a practical approximation to the 70:10:20 forecast, not a guarantee.</p>
<p>The reverse also matters. If demand moves toward higher-margin totes, the average contribution rises. Before cheering a lower break-even unit count, check whether the tote's production time, supplier capacity or stock availability can actually support the new plan.</p>
<div class="article-table-scroll"><table>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Illustrative scenario</th>
<th>Mug / tote / candle unit mix</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Weighted contribution</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Approximate total units to cover $7,000</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Original plan</td>
<td>50% / 30% / 20%</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$14.00</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">500</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>More mugs</td>
<td>70% / 10% / 20%</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$12.40</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">565, then verify whole units</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>More totes</td>
<td>30% / 50% / 20%</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$15.60</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">449, then verify whole units</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>At 30:50:20, $7,000 ÷ $15.60 is about 448.72, hence 449 units as a starting point. The table shows the effect of <strong>unit mix only</strong>. It assumes prices, per-item costs and shared fixed costs stay the same.</p>
<h2 id="turn-the-threshold-into-a-useful-weekly-plan">Turn the threshold into a useful weekly plan</h2>
<p>Five hundred monthly items is about 125 in a four-week planning month, but actual months and selling days vary. Check your own calendar and channels. If the shop has 20 selling days in a month, the original plan asks for 25 sales per selling day on average. If it can reliably make or source only 15 totes a week, the 150-tote monthly plan needs a capacity review before it becomes a target.</p>
<p>Add a profit target separately. To aim for $1,400 operating profit while keeping the $14 weighted contribution, calculate ($7,000 + $1,400) ÷ $14 = <strong>600 sales</strong>. At the original mix, that is 300 mugs, 180 totes and 120 candles. Recheck whether promotion costs or overtime needed to reach 600 sales change the variable cost or fixed cost assumptions.</p>
<p>For a new business without reliable sales history, use a range of mixes rather than presenting one guess as fact. Save three scenarios: current best estimate, more low-margin sales and more high-margin sales. Compare each scenario with inventory, customer demand and cash needed to buy stock. When the order mix settles, replace the forecast with actual trailing sales and rerun it monthly.</p>
<h2 id="use-the-calculators-without-losing-the-mix">Use the calculators without losing the mix</h2>
<p>Our <a href="https://mybreakeven.com/#calculator">general break-even calculator</a> works from a selling price, a variable cost and fixed expenses. If you want to model the <em>whole</em> product line with one representative sale, first calculate the weighted average price and variable cost for your assumed mix. In this example, weighted price is $32: ($30 × 50%) + ($40 × 30%) + ($25 × 20%). Weighted variable cost is $18: ($18 × 50%) + ($20 × 30%) + ($15 × 20%). Enter <strong>$32 price, $18 variable cost and $7,000 fixed cost</strong> to reproduce the $14 margin and 500-unit result. Label that unit as a modeled average sale, then verify each product quantity outside the calculator. Alternatively, model products separately to explore a single item's price change, but do not add separate fixed-cost break-even targets as though the business pays rent three times.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/blogs/fixed-vs-variable-costs/">fixed versus variable costs guide</a> helps when a cost could be allocated in several ways. If you want to understand the general single-product calculation first, see <a href="/blogs/break-even-formula-units-dollars/">the break-even formula</a>.</p>
<h2 id="mistakes-that-distort-the-answer">Mistakes that distort the answer</h2>
<p><strong>Using sales revenue shares instead of unit shares.</strong> The $14 calculation weights contributions by <em>number of items</em>, not by dollars sold. A $40 tote accounts for more revenue than a $25 candle even if one of each sells. If you have revenue-share data instead, use contribution-margin ratios and a revenue-based model; do not feed revenue percentages into the unit formula.</p>
<p><strong>Calling gross margin the contribution margin.</strong> Gross margin and variable-cost contribution can use different cost boundaries. A transaction fee or pick-and-pack charge may be missing from a product gross-margin report. Our <a href="/blogs/contribution-margin-vs-gross-margin/">gross margin versus contribution margin guide</a> explains the distinction.</p>
<p><strong>Applying one item's margin to all sales.</strong> That can overstate or understate the number of sales needed. At 500 all-mug sales, contribution is only $6,000, even though 500 sales at the planned mix cover $7,000.</p>
<p><strong>Ignoring stock and time.</strong> A spreadsheet may call for 180 totes when you can only make 100. Adjust production, price or the mix assumption; the arithmetic cannot create capacity.</p>
<p><strong>Treating break-even as cash in the bank.</strong> An item can be profitable on paper while a large stock purchase or delayed customer payment strains cash. Track inventory purchases and collection timing separately.</p>
<h2 id="faqs">FAQs</h2>
<h3 id="what-is-the-formula-for-multiple-product-break-even">What is the formula for multiple-product break-even?</h3>
<p>Total fixed costs ÷ sum of each product's (selling price − variable cost) × expected <strong>unit</strong> share. The result is total units at the assumed mix; round up and verify actual whole-product quantities.</p>
<h3 id="what-is-a-composite-unit">What is a composite unit?</h3>
<p>It is a bundle used for the calculation that mirrors your expected sales ratio. For a 5:3:2 mix, one composite unit represents five mugs, three totes and two candles. It need not be an actual bundle offered to shoppers.</p>
<h3 id="should-i-use-historical-or-forecast-sales-mix">Should I use historical or forecast sales mix?</h3>
<p>Use recent history if it still reflects what you sell now. Use a labeled forecast if you have changed products, prices or channels. Run a second mix scenario when the evidence is thin; revise using actual sales.</p>
<h3 id="can-i-calculate-break-even-revenue-instead-of-units">Can I calculate break-even revenue instead of units?</h3>
<p>Yes. Compute total expected sales dollars and contribution dollars for a representative mix, then divide fixed costs by the resulting weighted contribution-margin ratio. State clearly whether the output is dollars or units so you do not compare them as if they were interchangeable.</p>
<h3 id="does-a-discount-change-break-even-even-if-unit-mix-stays-the-same">Does a discount change break-even even if unit mix stays the same?</h3>
<p>Yes. A lower price reduces the discounted item's contribution unless a cost falls too. Recalculate the item's margin, weighted margin and required units. Also model any increase in order volume separately rather than assuming the promotion will deliver it.</p>
<h3 id="what-if-one-product-has-a-negative-contribution-margin">What if one product has a negative contribution margin?</h3>
<p>Every extra sale of that item, at the stated price and cost, reduces the total contribution before fixed costs. Investigate its costs, price and strategic reason for keeping it; do not use a profitable item's margin to hide the loss in the planning model.</p>
<h2 id="keep-these-numbers-on-one-page">Keep these numbers on one page</h2>
<ul>
<li>Track a contribution margin for each product using the same cost rules.</li>
<li>Base the weighted margin on expected <strong>unit</strong> sales mix and recalculate when that mix changes.</li>
<li>Translate the rounded total into whole-product targets, then check stock, time and cash.</li>
<li>Add target profit explicitly; break-even by itself pays the modeled costs and nothing extra.</li>
</ul>`,faq:[{q:`What is the formula for multiple-product break-even?`,a:`Total fixed costs ÷ sum of each product's (selling price − variable cost) × expected unit share. The result is total units at the assumed mix; round up and verify actual whole-product quantities.`},{q:`What is a composite unit?`,a:`It is a bundle used for the calculation that mirrors your expected sales ratio. For a 5:3:2 mix, one composite unit represents five mugs, three totes and two candles. It need not be an actual bundle offered to shoppers.`},{q:`Should I use historical or forecast sales mix?`,a:`Use recent history if it still reflects what you sell now. Use a labeled forecast if you have changed products, prices or channels. Run a second mix scenario when the evidence is thin; revise using actual sales.`},{q:`Can I calculate break-even revenue instead of units?`,a:`Yes. Compute total expected sales dollars and contribution dollars for a representative mix, then divide fixed costs by the resulting weighted contribution-margin ratio. State clearly whether the output is dollars or units so you do not compare them as if they were interchangeable.`},{q:`Does a discount change break-even even if unit mix stays the same?`,a:`Yes. A lower price reduces the discounted item's contribution unless a cost falls too. Recalculate the item's margin, weighted margin and required units. Also model any increase in order volume separately rather than assuming the promotion will deliver it.`},{q:`What if one product has a negative contribution margin?`,a:`Every extra sale of that item, at the stated price and cost, reduces the total contribution before fixed costs. Investigate its costs, price and strategic reason for keeping it; do not use a profitable item's margin to hide the loss in the planning model.`}]},{slug:`post-construction-cleaning-estimate`,tag:`Cleaning`,name:`Cleaning Business`,cluster:`pricing`,title:`How to Estimate a Post-Construction Cleaning Job`,seoTitle:`Post-Construction Cleaning Job Estimate`,description:`Estimate a post-construction clean from a site walk, crew hours, supplies, access and scope. Follow a worked bid with a practical client checklist.`,metaDescription:`Estimate a post-construction clean from a site walk, crew hours, supplies, access and scope. Follow a worked bid with a practical client checklist.`,image:`/images/blog/post-construction-cleaning-estimate-editorial.webp`,alt:`Professional cleaner planning work in a dusty newly renovated office with a vacuum nearby`,imageCaption:`Illustrative editorial image; example costs are assumptions, not market prices.`,published:`2026-09-29`,modified:`2026-09-29`,opening:`The contractor says the renovation is finished. You arrive to find dust on the window tracks, tape on the glass and a second trade still cutting trim. A simple square-foot quote cannot tell you how many times you will clean the same room. Before you give a price, decide exactly what is ready, what needs doing and when the client will accept the work.`,unit:`jobs`,singular:`job`,calculatorSlug:`cleaning-business-break-even-calculator`,tags:[`Cleaning`,`Post-Construction`,`Estimating`],features:[`A checked worked example`,`Clearly stated assumptions`,`Practical capacity and risk checks`],html:`<p>The contractor says the renovation is finished. You arrive to find dust on the window tracks, tape on the glass and a second trade still cutting trim. A simple square-foot quote cannot tell you how many times you will clean the same room. Before you give a price, decide exactly <strong>what is ready, what needs doing and when the client will accept the work</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Walk the space after the trades have defined their finish date. List the cleaning stages and surfaces, estimate paid <em>cleaner-hours</em> by task, add direct supplies and travel, reserve time for a defined final walkthrough, then add allocated overhead and price for your target margin. Put exclusions and a change-order rule in writing. A fictional 2,000-square-foot office example below costs $1,154 to deliver and support; at a 20% margin its quote is <strong>$1,442.50</strong>, before applicable tax. Your own bid may differ substantially.</p>
<p>This guide is for an ordinary <strong>one-time post-construction or post-renovation clean</strong>. It is distinct from pricing a recurring office contract. The square footage in the example describes the site; it is not a universal rate card.</p>
<h2 id="walk-the-job-before-pricing-it">Walk the job before pricing it</h2>
<p>Ask when construction dust-generating work will stop and when the area will be released to the cleaning crew. Photograph the condition with permission. Note floor types, number of rooms, bathrooms, kitchens, interior glazing, high shelves, vents or fixtures within safe reach, adhesive residue, paint specks, access hours, parking, elevator or stair travel, water and power. Count items, not just floor area: ten new cabinets and their drawers may take more detail time than an open room of the same size.</p>
<p>Clarify what the contractor has already removed. Normal service might include collecting light leftover debris, dust capture on reachable surfaces, cleaning interior glass, wiping fixtures and wet cleaning suitable floors. Heavy rubble, demolition waste, paint removal, grout restoration, duct cleaning and high-access work can require different crews, equipment or separate quotes. Identify who is responsible for disposal fees and dumpsters. An occupied building may require noise restrictions, security check-in or working around other trades. Every one of those conditions affects paid time.</p>
<p>Most importantly, ask whether this is a <strong>rough clean</strong>, a <strong>final clean</strong>, or a separate <strong>touch-up after punch-list work</strong>. Rough work can address debris and accessible surfaces before final finishes. Final work targets fine dust and presentation after the trades finish. A touch-up is a return visit for fingerprints and dust created after that final clean. Do not promise all three for one price unless you have allocated hours for all three and established the handoff dates.</p>
<h2 id="convert-the-task-list-into-cleaner-hours">Convert the task list into cleaner-hours</h2>
<p>Use your own job records or a room-by-room time trial for the base estimate. A pair of cleaners on site for four hours is <strong>eight cleaner-hours</strong>. Add paid loading, setup, travel between locations and a handover where relevant. Say whether the work can happen in one visit or requires separate trips. In the example below, assume the contractor has removed major debris and finished dust-generating work. The final clean and one short sign-off visit are included; new dust from later trades is an approved extra.</p>
<div class="article-table-scroll"><table>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Illustrative work package</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Paid cleaner-hours</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Prep, setup and protection check</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">2</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Detail dust capture on reachable ledges, cabinets and fixtures</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">10</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Interior glass, frames and tracks</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">6</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Bathrooms and kitchenette</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">5</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Floors and finish-safe spot work</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">6</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Walkthrough, touch-ups and closeout</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">3</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td><strong>Total</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: right;"><strong>32</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>At an <strong>illustrative fully loaded labor cost of $28 per paid cleaner-hour</strong>, labor is 32 × $28 = <strong>$896</strong>. “Fully loaded” here means the business's budgeted cost of employing that hour, not the employee's take-home pay. Do not treat $28 as a national labor rate. Replace it with your own payroll, employer costs, paid travel rules and staffing setup.</p>
<p>Now add $78 of supplies and consumables, $55 for travel and parking, $45 for equipment use and maintenance, and $80 of allocated supervision and operating overhead. The total estimated job cost is <strong>$896 + $78 + $55 + $45 + $80 = $1,154</strong>. These are invented example inputs, not a market quote.</p>
<p>To target a <strong>20% margin on the quoted price</strong>, divide $1,154 by (1 − 0.20): <strong>$1,442.50</strong>. The modeled difference of $288.50 is 20% of the quote. Adding 20% <em>to cost</em> would be $1,384.80 and yield a lower margin, so make clear which method you use. Check whether local sales tax applies to your service separately; the calculation above excludes tax.</p>
<p>For internal comparison only, $1,442.50 divided by 2,000 square feet is about <strong>$0.72 per square foot</strong>. That number is the <em>output</em> of this inspected scope and these assumptions. Copying it onto a different site with a different amount of glass, residue or rework would be a bad bid.</p>
<h2 id="price-the-uncertainty-you-can-actually-describe">Price the uncertainty you can actually describe</h2>
<p>Suppose the final clean takes <strong>six additional paid cleaner-hours</strong> because adhesive remains on accessible glass. At the same $28 cost, direct labor rises by $168. If all other costs stay constant, cost becomes <strong>$1,322</strong>. At the original $1,442.50 price, modeled profit falls to <strong>$120.50</strong>, an 8.35% margin. To preserve 20%, the revised price would be <strong>$1,652.50</strong> ($1,322 ÷ 0.80), subject to a scope change the client approves. The $210 price difference is a change-order example, not permission to bill an unexpected charge without agreement.</p>
<p>Instead of hiding unlimited “contingency” in the quote, name the triggers: new debris after handoff, another trade working during the clean, adhesive beyond the inspected area, no running water, delayed access or a second return trip. State the included number of visits and the price method for approved extra cleaner-hours or a revised fixed price. If you know a condition is likely, put realistic time for it in the base scope.</p>
<div class="article-table-scroll"><table>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Bid check</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Base case</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Six extra cleaner-hours at unchanged price</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Approved revised scope at 20% margin</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Modeled job cost</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$1,154</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$1,322</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$1,322</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Client price before tax</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$1,442.50</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$1,442.50</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$1,652.50</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Modeled margin</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">20%</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">8.35%</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">20%</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The numbers make the operational issue visible: a little extra labor can consume much of a one-time job's planned profit. Recording <em>why</em> the hours changed will improve your next estimate far more than copying a competitor's square-foot price.</p>
<h2 id="treat-fine-dust-and-possible-hazards-as-scope-decisions">Treat fine dust and possible hazards as scope decisions</h2>
<p>Construction dust can be different from ordinary office dust. If tasks could expose workers to respirable crystalline silica, OSHA's <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1926/1926.1153">construction silica standard</a> limits dry sweeping or dry brushing when it contributes to exposure, except where alternatives such as wet methods or HEPA-filtered vacuuming are not feasible. Choose equipment and work practices based on the actual material and applicable requirements; a household vacuum or a quick dry broom is not a universal solution.</p>
<p>If a renovation disturbed painted surfaces in a pre-1978 home or child-occupied facility, stop and clarify responsibility before quoting an ordinary final clean. EPA's <a href="https://www.epa.gov/lead/renovation-repair-and-painting-program-work-practices">Renovation, Repair and Painting work-practices page</a> describes containment, cleanup and verification requirements for covered work. Your general cleaning bid should not claim to perform lead-safe verification or hazardous-material remediation unless the appropriately qualified team and required procedure are actually in scope. The same principle applies to suspected asbestos or unknown contamination: have the responsible contractor assess it, then define the service you can safely deliver.</p>
<p>Also protect new finishes. Verify the manufacturer's instructions for flooring, stone, glass coatings and cabinetry before selecting chemicals or abrasive pads. A fast clean that damages a new surface is expensive for everyone. Put unusual residue removal or surface repair on a separate approved scope.</p>
<h2 id="give-the-client-a-quote-they-can-compare-and-accept">Give the client a quote they can compare and accept</h2>
<p>A usable proposal names the property, date of walkthrough, areas and surfaces, included tasks, number of passes or visits, target access dates, conditions required for the site to be ready, supplies and equipment, disposal responsibility, exclusions, price before tax, payment terms and who approves extra work. Define acceptance: a joint walkthrough or written punch list within an agreed period after completion. A client can then see what the price covers and when your team is done.</p>
<p>For example, describe “interior glazing and tracks accessible from the floor” rather than “all windows.” Describe “dust capture on reachable finished surfaces after trades vacate” rather than “remove all construction dust forever.” Be specific without burying the customer in a legal document. If the builder needs a rough clean now and final clean after inspection, list two visits and price the second trip's travel and setup.</p>
<p>Keep a short job record afterward: estimated versus actual cleaner-hours by task, materials used, number of returns and what caused rework. Use it to refine the next bid. Our <a href="/blogs/commercial-cleaning-contract-bid/">commercial cleaning contract bidding guide</a> addresses monthly recurring work; its visit-frequency model should not be copied onto this one-time project. If you need a baseline for the business's monthly survival target, use the <a href="https://mybreakeven.com/calculators/cleaning-business-break-even-calculator/">cleaning business break-even calculator</a> with your average job contribution, fixed expenses and owner pay. The calculator tests <em>monthly business volume</em>; this site-walk worksheet sets the <strong>individual job quote</strong>. See also <a href="/blogs/cleaning-business-monthly-expenses/">cleaning business monthly expenses</a> when assigning overhead.</p>
<h2 id="common-estimating-misses">Common estimating misses</h2>
<p><strong>Using square footage as the whole scope.</strong> A 2,000-square-foot shell and a finished 2,000-square-foot office have different numbers of ledges, cabinets, bathrooms and glass panels. Count them.</p>
<p><strong>Forgetting the second visit.</strong> A rough clean before final trim work may need a full final clean later. Count both loading and travel as well as on-site hours.</p>
<p><strong>Confusing crew time with payroll hours.</strong> Two people spending six hours each is 12 paid cleaner-hours. Price labor at the full 12, plus paid setup or travel.</p>
<p><strong>Leaving overhead and owner work out.</strong> Scheduling, walk-throughs, insurance, equipment upkeep and administration have costs even on a one-time job. Allocate them consistently, then check your desired margin.</p>
<p><strong>Promising hazard removal in a normal clean.</strong> Unknown dust or old painted surfaces warrant a scope and safety check before work begins. Put any specialist work with the appropriately qualified provider.</p>
<p><strong>Assuming construction is finished because the date says so.</strong> Confirm the actual release of the area and specify how later trade activity is handled.</p>
<h2 id="faqs">FAQs</h2>
<h3 id="how-much-should-i-charge-per-square-foot-for-post-construction-cleaning">How much should I charge per square foot for post-construction cleaning?</h3>
<p>There is no single price that fits every site. Inspect tasks and surfaces, estimate cleaner-hours and direct expenses, then add allocated overhead and your target margin. Divide the resulting quote by area only as a cross-check against your own completed jobs with similar scope.</p>
<h3 id="how-do-i-estimate-labor-hours-before-i-have-job-history">How do I estimate labor hours before I have job history?</h3>
<p>Break the site into rooms and tasks, time a small representative area if access is allowed, and record assumptions. Include setup, movement between areas, final checks and likely touch-ups. Compare the first job's actual hours with the estimate and improve the template.</p>
<h3 id="is-rough-cleaning-the-same-as-final-cleaning">Is rough cleaning the same as final cleaning?</h3>
<p>No. A rough clean generally happens earlier, while the final clean prepares finished surfaces for handover. The exact definitions vary by project, so list tasks and timing for each pass in your proposal.</p>
<h3 id="do-i-include-debris-removal-and-a-dumpster">Do I include debris removal and a dumpster?</h3>
<p>Only if you have inspected the debris, can handle it safely and have priced labor, transport and disposal. State whether the builder removes construction waste and who pays any fees.</p>
<h3 id="what-happens-if-workers-make-new-dust-after-i-finish">What happens if workers make new dust after I finish?</h3>
<p>Set a handoff time and acceptance process. If later trade activity requires a return, define it as an approved extra visit or separately priced touch-up, with access and payment terms agreed beforehand.</p>
<h3 id="how-does-a-20-margin-differ-from-a-20-markup">How does a 20% margin differ from a 20% markup?</h3>
<p>Margin uses client price as its denominator; markup uses cost. On $1,154 of modeled cost, a 20% margin requires $1,442.50. A 20% markup gives $1,384.80, which is about a 16.67% margin.</p>
<h2 id="takeaways-for-the-next-walkthrough">Takeaways for the next walkthrough</h2>
<ul>
<li>Write down the actual surfaces, stages and site-readiness conditions before pricing.</li>
<li>Count paid cleaner-hours for the full crew, direct costs and allocated overhead.</li>
<li>Check dust and material risks before assigning ordinary cleaning methods.</li>
<li>Show the included visits, exclusions and change-order triggers in the quote.</li>
<li>Compare estimated and actual hours afterward so your next bid uses evidence from your own work.</li>
</ul>`,faq:[{q:`How much should I charge per square foot for post-construction cleaning?`,a:`There is no single price that fits every site. Inspect tasks and surfaces, estimate cleaner-hours and direct expenses, then add allocated overhead and your target margin. Divide the resulting quote by area only as a cross-check against your own completed jobs with similar scope.`},{q:`How do I estimate labor hours before I have job history?`,a:`Break the site into rooms and tasks, time a small representative area if access is allowed, and record assumptions. Include setup, movement between areas, final checks and likely touch-ups. Compare the first job's actual hours with the estimate and improve the template.`},{q:`Is rough cleaning the same as final cleaning?`,a:`No. A rough clean generally happens earlier, while the final clean prepares finished surfaces for handover. The exact definitions vary by project, so list tasks and timing for each pass in your proposal.`},{q:`Do I include debris removal and a dumpster?`,a:`Only if you have inspected the debris, can handle it safely and have priced labor, transport and disposal. State whether the builder removes construction waste and who pays any fees.`},{q:`What happens if workers make new dust after I finish?`,a:`Set a handoff time and acceptance process. If later trade activity requires a return, define it as an approved extra visit or separately priced touch-up, with access and payment terms agreed beforehand.`},{q:`How does a 20% margin differ from a 20% markup?`,a:`Margin uses client price as its denominator; markup uses cost. On $1,154 of modeled cost, a 20% margin requires $1,442.50. A 20% markup gives $1,384.80, which is about a 16.67% margin.`}]},{slug:`landscaping-job-costing-estimate-vs-actual`,tag:`Landscaping`,name:`Landscaping`,cluster:`profitability`,title:`Landscaping Job Costing: Estimate vs Actual`,seoTitle:`Landscaping Job Costing: Estimate vs Actual`,description:`Compare a landscaping job estimate with actual crew hours, materials and equipment costs. Follow a checked example to price the next job better.`,metaDescription:`Compare a landscaping job estimate with actual crew hours, materials and equipment costs. Follow a checked example to price the next job better.`,image:`/images/blog/landscaping-job-costing-estimate-vs-actual-editorial.webp`,alt:`Landscaping crew lead checks a clipboard beside a newly planted bed and work tools`,imageCaption:`Illustrative editorial image; example dollars are planning assumptions, not industry averages.`,published:`2026-09-29`,modified:`2026-09-29`,opening:`The crew finishes a planting job, the customer pays the agreed $2,400, and the calendar is full. Did the job earn the $547 you expected? An invoice cannot tell you. You need the hours, materials, equipment and trips that actually went into that address, then you can compare the result with the estimate you used to set the quote.`,unit:`jobs`,singular:`job`,calculatorSlug:`landscaping-break-even-calculator`,tags:[`Landscaping`,`Job Costing`,`Profitability`],features:[`A checked worked example`,`Clearly stated assumptions`,`Practical capacity and risk checks`],html:`<p>The crew finishes a planting job, the customer pays the agreed $2,400, and the calendar is full. Did the job earn the $547 you expected? An invoice cannot tell you. You need the hours, materials, equipment and trips that actually went into that address, then you can compare the result with the estimate you used to set the quote.</p>
<p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Record estimated and actual costs in the same categories. Subtract actual job cost from the agreed price, then compare actual profit with estimated profit. In the fictional example below, a job quoted at $2,400 was expected to cost $1,853 and actually cost $2,160. Profit fell from <strong>$547 to $240</strong>. The $307 gap points to specific estimating errors you can correct on the next bid.</p>
<p>This is a method for <strong>one completed job</strong>. Our <a href="/blogs/landscaping-route-density-profit/">landscaping route-density guide</a> handles time lost between recurring stops; a route can look profitable in aggregate while one installation job quietly runs long.</p>
<h2 id="use-the-same-cost-categories-before-and-after-the-job">Use the same cost categories before and after the job</h2>
<p>Think of a small residential planting and mulch project. The written scope includes preparing beds, installing plants and mulch, cleaning up and hauling ordinary green waste. A team has walked the site and quoted $2,400. All prices, wage costs and times below are <strong>invented inputs for the calculation</strong>, not US averages or recommendations for your market.</p>
<div class="article-table-scroll"><table>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Cost category</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Quote estimate</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Actual job record</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Difference</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Crew labor</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">24 paid crew-hours × $32 = $768</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">30 hours × $32 = $960</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">+$192</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Plants and mulch</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$650</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$730</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">+$80</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Equipment use</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$110</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$125</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">+$15</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Disposal</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$75</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$75</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$0</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Job travel</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$90</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$110</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">+$20</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Allocated overhead and supervision</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$160</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$160</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$0</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td><strong>Total cost</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: right;"><strong>$1,853</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: right;"><strong>$2,160</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: right;"><strong>+$307</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The $32 is a <em>fully loaded paid crew-hour cost assumption</em>: it should reflect your budget for wages and employer costs, not a universal hourly wage. If two employees spend three hours each at the site, record <strong>six crew-hours</strong>, not three clock hours. If the owner works on the job, value that production time consistently as well.</p>
<p>The cost reconciliation is exact: $960 − $768 = $192 extra labor; $730 − $650 = $80 extra materials; $125 − $110 = $15 equipment; and $110 − $90 = $20 travel. Together, those four movements are <strong>$307</strong>. Price stayed $2,400, so estimated operating profit was $2,400 − $1,853 = <strong>$547</strong>, or 22.79% of the quoted price. Actual operating profit was $2,400 − $2,160 = <strong>$240</strong>, or 10%. That is a smaller margin even though the job was still profitable on these assumptions.</p>
<p>Allocated overhead helps assess full job profitability, but it is <strong>not</strong> cash spent anew on each visit. For a monthly break-even calculation, keep shared overhead in monthly fixed costs and leave the $160 allocation out of per-job variable cost so you do not count it twice.</p>
<h2 id="find-the-operational-cause-behind-each-variance">Find the operational cause behind each variance</h2>
<p>The variance table says <em>what</em> changed, not <em>why</em>. If the extra six crew-hours came from hidden roots, a delayed plant delivery or a route that required two trips, each calls for a different next step. Write the cause beside the time entry while the job is still fresh. “Labor +$192” by itself will not improve the next quote.</p>
<p>For labor, compare estimated tasks with actual tasks. Was the bed larger than the marked area? Did a customer request a different edge? Did the team spend an unplanned hour collecting materials? Separate normal rework from approved extra work. If the scope changed, preserve the customer's approval and the extra hours as a change order; do not quietly change the invoice after the fact.</p>
<p>For materials, keep purchase quantities separate from consumption. A truck may carry $900 of stock but leave $730 on this job. Count leftover plants and unopened mulch back into inventory; do not charge the full supplier receipt to the property if some material is used elsewhere. Record damaged or wasted material separately. For equipment, decide whether you use an hourly rate, a per-job allowance or actual rental charges, then apply the same rule in estimate and actual. Include delivery and fuel if they are genuinely job-specific.</p>
<p>Travel can be a surprisingly visible variance on small jobs. The estimate allowed $90; the job record shows $110. Identify whether that $20 came from a supplier trip, a second disposal run or a site access delay. Our <a href="/blogs/lawn-care-pricing-per-acre/">lawn-care pricing guide</a> addresses setting rates across routine services; this reconciliation is what tests whether an individual quote paid for what you delivered.</p>
<h2 id="three-ways-the-same-quote-can-finish">Three ways the same quote can finish</h2>
<p>Holding the $2,400 customer price fixed shows why an apparently small overrun matters:</p>
<div class="article-table-scroll"><table>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Illustrative finish</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Job cost</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Modeled profit</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Margin on $2,400</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Work matches estimate</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$1,853</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$547</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">22.79%</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Recorded actual job</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$2,160</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$240</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">10%</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Six more paid crew-hours beyond actual, with other costs unchanged</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$2,352</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$48</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">2%</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The third case adds 6 × $32 = $192 to the recorded actual cost. If that additional time is a customer-approved scope change, quote the added work under your change-order terms. If it is your estimating error, use it as a lesson for the next bid instead of assuming the customer will fund it retrospectively.</p>
<p>For a future job that <em>really has</em> the $2,160 cost structure, a 20% target margin on the selling price requires $2,160 ÷ 0.80 = <strong>$2,700</strong>. That is a future planning price, not a claim that the completed client owes $300 extra. It also assumes demand and scope can support that price. If the site is easier or materials cheaper, recalculate from that job's own takeoff.</p>
<h2 id="make-your-job-record-usable-not-elaborate">Make your job record usable, not elaborate</h2>
<p>Create one line per cost category with estimated quantity and rate, then actual quantity and rate. Crew timesheets should carry a job ID and task: bed prep, planting, mulch, loading, travel, disposal and punch-list return. Supplier receipts should be tagged to the job only for the quantity consumed. A photo of site conditions taken with permission and a dated scope note can explain the variance months later.</p>
<p>Close the job within a few days. Ask the crew lead which unplanned task consumed the most time, compare the approved scope with the delivered work and note what to change in the estimating template. After several comparable jobs, use a median or sensible range of <strong>your own recorded crew-hours per task</strong>, not a one-size-fits-all internet rate. Rain, steep access, soil condition, plant size and distance from the supplier can shift a particular property out of that range.</p>
<p>Also check the calendar. A revised quote might have enough dollar margin but require more crew-hours than you can schedule during the planting season. The <a href="/blogs/landscaping-seasonal-break-even/">landscaping seasonal break-even guide</a> helps with the slower months; this job-cost record helps you see whether the busy month's booked work actually contributes enough.</p>
<h2 id="check-business-wide-break-even-separately">Check business-wide break-even separately</h2>
<p>Your completed job's profit and your business's monthly break-even answer different questions. In the example, <strong>direct job cost before allocated overhead</strong> was $2,000 ($2,160 − $160). The $2,400 price therefore left $400 toward shared monthly costs. The $160 allocation was only a reporting convention; the actual monthly overhead budget may be higher or lower than this single-job share.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="https://mybreakeven.com/calculators/landscaping-break-even-calculator/">landscaping business break-even calculator</a> with price per comparable job, job-level variable cost, real monthly fixed costs, owner salary and realistic crew capacity. For this illustrative job, do not enter both $2,160 as variable cost <em>and</em> the $160 of overhead again as fixed cost. The calculator accepts other currencies; the US-dollar example is simply easier to audit.</p>
<h2 id="mistakes-that-make-job-costing-look-better-than-it-is">Mistakes that make job costing look better than it is</h2>
<p><strong>Counting onsite elapsed time instead of paid crew-hours.</strong> Three people working four hours cost twelve paid hours, even if the visit appears as a four-hour calendar block.</p>
<p><strong>Booking a full delivery as one job's materials.</strong> Leftover stock belongs to inventory or another job, while damaged material should be recorded where the loss occurred.</p>
<p><strong>Leaving owner labor invisible.</strong> If you performed the install, estimate what comparable paid labor would cost and state whether owner pay is included.</p>
<p><strong>Moving costs between jobs to make a quote look successful.</strong> A second supply run, equipment pickup or punch-list visit belongs in the job record if it was needed to deliver this scope.</p>
<p><strong>Using allocated overhead twice.</strong> Decide whether the number is part of an individual full-cost report or monthly fixed costs in a break-even model. Do not combine both in the same calculation.</p>
<p><strong>Treating the variance as permission to change a signed price.</strong> Use the written scope and approved change process. The completed record mainly improves future bids.</p>
<h2 id="faqs">FAQs</h2>
<h3 id="what-is-the-formula-for-landscaping-job-costing">What is the formula for landscaping job costing?</h3>
<p>For a completed job, add paid crew labor, consumed materials, equipment, disposal, travel and the chosen overhead allocation. Subtract total cost from the client price to find modeled job profit. Keep direct job costs separate when using a monthly break-even calculator.</p>
<h3 id="should-i-calculate-labor-by-crew-hours-or-onsite-hours">Should I calculate labor by crew-hours or onsite hours?</h3>
<p>Use paid crew-hours: number of paid people multiplied by hours each works, including paid setup or travel that belongs to the job. Onsite wall-clock time alone understates labor when several employees work together.</p>
<h3 id="what-if-plants-were-purchased-but-never-installed">What if plants were purchased but never installed?</h3>
<p>If usable plants remain in stock, remove their cost from this job and put it in inventory. If they were damaged or discarded because of this job, record the loss and its cause.</p>
<h3 id="how-do-i-account-for-a-subcontractor">How do I account for a subcontractor?</h3>
<p>Put the subcontractor's agreed job-specific charge and any related supervision or travel in the estimate and actual record. Confirm the delivered scope and invoice before closing the job.</p>
<h3 id="is-a-10-job-margin-the-same-as-a-10-business-profit-margin">Is a 10% job margin the same as a 10% business profit margin?</h3>
<p>No. A job margin depends on which expenses you allocated to it. The business also pays shared expenses, slower-season costs and taxes; reconcile those at the period level.</p>
<h3 id="when-should-i-update-my-estimating-template">When should I update my estimating template?</h3>
<p>Review every substantial overrun promptly, then look for a repeated cause across similar jobs. Change the relevant task hours, waste allowance, supplier assumptions or access question rather than adding an unexplained blanket percentage.</p>
<h2 id="keep-from-this-job">Keep from this job</h2>
<ul>
<li>The $307 overrun is traceable to labor, materials, equipment and travel; the next estimate can address each cause.</li>
<li>Record actual quantities and paid crew-hours against the same categories used in the quote.</li>
<li>Separate job contribution from allocated overhead when moving to monthly break-even.</li>
<li>Use finished-job evidence and crew capacity to set the next price, not the invoice total alone.</li>
</ul>`,faq:[{q:`What is the formula for landscaping job costing?`,a:`For a completed job, add paid crew labor, consumed materials, equipment, disposal, travel and the chosen overhead allocation. Subtract total cost from the client price to find modeled job profit. Keep direct job costs separate when using a monthly break-even calculator.`},{q:`Should I calculate labor by crew-hours or onsite hours?`,a:`Use paid crew-hours: number of paid people multiplied by hours each works, including paid setup or travel that belongs to the job. Onsite wall-clock time alone understates labor when several employees work together.`},{q:`What if plants were purchased but never installed?`,a:`If usable plants remain in stock, remove their cost from this job and put it in inventory. If they were damaged or discarded because of this job, record the loss and its cause.`},{q:`How do I account for a subcontractor?`,a:`Put the subcontractor's agreed job-specific charge and any related supervision or travel in the estimate and actual record. Confirm the delivered scope and invoice before closing the job.`},{q:`Is a 10% job margin the same as a 10% business profit margin?`,a:`No. A job margin depends on which expenses you allocated to it. The business also pays shared expenses, slower-season costs and taxes; reconcile those at the period level.`},{q:`When should I update my estimating template?`,a:`Review every substantial overrun promptly, then look for a repeated cause across similar jobs. Change the relevant task hours, waste allowance, supplier assumptions or access question rather than adding an unexplained blanket percentage.`}]},{slug:`photography-mini-session-profit`,tag:`Photography`,name:`Photography`,cluster:`profitability`,title:`Are Photography Mini Sessions Profitable?`,seoTitle:`Photography Mini Session Profit Guide`,description:`Price photography mini sessions with booked slots, editing, owner time and event costs. See a checked example and the bookings needed to cover the day.`,metaDescription:`Price photography mini sessions with booked slots, editing, owner time and event costs. See a checked example and the bookings needed to cover the day.`,image:`/images/blog/photography-mini-session-profit-editorial.webp`,alt:`Portrait photographer reviews an appointment clipboard in a small daylight studio`,imageCaption:`Illustrative editorial image; the time and dollar figures in this guide are modeled assumptions.`,published:`2026-09-29`,modified:`2026-09-29`,opening:`Eight 15-minute portraits on one Saturday sound like efficient work. But a four-hour camera block can generate another six hours of editing and client messages. A mini-session event is profitable when its booked slots cover the day's setup, space, promotion, delivery and your planned pay for every hour, with enough room for cancellations or retouches.`,unit:`sessions`,singular:`session`,calculatorSlug:`photography-business-break-even-calculator`,tags:[`Photography`,`Mini Sessions`,`Pricing`],features:[`A checked worked example`,`Clearly stated assumptions`,`Practical capacity and risk checks`],html:`<p>Eight 15-minute portraits on one Saturday sound like efficient work. But a four-hour camera block can generate another six hours of editing and client messages. A mini-session event is profitable when its <strong>booked slots cover the day's setup, space, promotion, delivery and your planned pay for every hour</strong>, with enough room for cancellations or retouches.</p>
<p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Set a price per booked slot, subtract the cost that increases with each booking, and budget the event costs and owner hours that happen even if bookings are light. In the illustrative eight-slot event below, four bookings cover the modeled cost of the day; eight bookings leave <strong>$790 after planned owner pay</strong>. This is an example for the math, not a typical photographer's rate or a promise of demand.</p>
<p>This guide is about a <strong>short, fixed-date portrait event</strong>. A full custom shoot has different travel, scope and editing assumptions; our <a href="/blogs/photography-cost-per-session-editing/">photography cost-per-session guide</a> covers that broader session calculation.</p>
<h2 id="define-what-a-mini-session-actually-includes">Define what a mini-session actually includes</h2>
<p>Say you offer a 15-minute portrait, one backdrop, a specified number of edited digital files and online delivery. You schedule a 15-minute buffer between appointments, giving each customer a <strong>30-minute calendar slot</strong>. Eight slots fill four hours on site. Written terms say when the gallery will be delivered, how many selections are included, what retouching means, and how rescheduling or missed appointments work. An unlimited gallery or repeated revisions turns a short shoot into an unpredictable editing job.</p>
<p>Count more than time behind the camera. You also prepare the location, set lights, test the background, check bookings and send the finished files. In this <strong>fictional USD example</strong>, one hour is reserved for setup and breakdown, four hours for the entire on-site slot block, and each booking needs 30 minutes of editing plus 15 minutes of selection and delivery administration. The owner plans to pay themselves <strong>$50 per working hour</strong>. That is an assumption about this business, not a published US wage benchmark.</p>
<div class="article-table-scroll"><table>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Planning item</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Example assumption</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Client price per reserved slot</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$225</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Available slots</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">8 × 30 minutes = 4 hours</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Event setup and on-site block</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">1 + 4 = 5 owner-hours</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Editing and delivery per booking</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">0.75 owner-hours</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Planned owner-pay cost</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$50 per hour</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Booking-specific fee and gallery cost</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$10 per booking</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Studio, assistant and event advertising</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$160 + $100 + $120 = $380</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>For eight booked sessions, owner time is 5 fixed event-hours plus 8 × 0.75 delivery-hours = <strong>11 hours</strong>. Planned owner pay is <strong>$550</strong>. Sales are 8 × $225 = <strong>$1,800</strong>. Booking-specific costs are 8 × $10 = <strong>$80</strong>. Add $380 of event costs: total modeled cost is $550 + $80 + $380 = <strong>$1,010</strong>, leaving <strong>$790</strong> after the owner's planned pay. The resulting surplus is $790 ÷ $1,800 = <strong>43.89% of sales</strong> under these assumptions. It is not a market-average photography margin.</p>
<p>If the owner does the work but pays no separate wage, the business bank account could show more cash left after the event. That is not the same as a sustainable profit after compensating 11 hours of work. Decide which question you are answering and label the result. For ordinary package choices, our <a href="/blogs/photography-pricing-packages/">photography pricing-packages guide</a> explains how deliverables and revisions change the quote.</p>
<h2 id="find-the-booking-count-that-pays-for-the-day">Find the booking count that pays for the day</h2>
<p>Treat the studio, assistant and event advertising ($380) plus the five owner-hours reserved whether three or eight people attend ($250) as a <strong>$630 event base</strong>. Each booking then adds $10 of per-client cost and 0.75 hours × $50 = $37.50 of editing/admin pay. At $225 per slot, incremental contribution is <strong>$225 − $10 − $37.50 = $177.50</strong>.</p>
<p>Break-even booked slots = $630 ÷ $177.50 = <strong>3.55</strong>, rounded up to <strong>four complete paid bookings</strong>. At four, contribution is $710, leaving $80 after the $630 event base. That tells you what bookings <em>would</em> cover the modeled day. It does not imply four people will book or that four will pay under an unclear cancellation policy.</p>
<div class="article-table-scroll"><table>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th style="text-align: right;">Paid bookings</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Sales</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Owner time and planned pay</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Other modeled costs</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Surplus after owner pay</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td style="text-align: right;">3</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$675</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">7.25 hours = $362.50</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$380 + $30</td>
<td style="text-align: right;"><strong>−$97.50</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td style="text-align: right;">4</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$900</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">8 hours = $400</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$380 + $40</td>
<td style="text-align: right;"><strong>$80</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td style="text-align: right;">6</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$1,350</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">9.5 hours = $475</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$380 + $60</td>
<td style="text-align: right;"><strong>$435</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td style="text-align: right;">8</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$1,800</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">11 hours = $550</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$380 + $80</td>
<td style="text-align: right;"><strong>$790</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>All rows keep the four-hour slot block reserved even when some slots go unsold. If you can close an empty portion of the schedule and genuinely release paid hours or studio time, make a second model with those lower costs. Do not assume you can reclaim a full-day studio rental merely because three clients did not book.</p>
<h2 id="what-changes-a-mini-session-events-result">What changes a mini-session event's result?</h2>
<p><strong>Editing scope.</strong> If each of eight bookings needs an <em>extra</em> 30 minutes of retouching, the owner works four more hours. At the assumed $50 owner-pay rate, surplus falls from $790 to <strong>$590</strong>. This is why the deliverables and revision policy belong in the sales page, not in a private mental note.</p>
<p><strong>No-shows, refunds and reschedules.</strong> A reserved slot with no payment leaves the on-site block in place and can still generate admin time. Model booked, <em>paid</em> and completed slots separately. A deposit or rescheduling term can help you plan, but apply your actual policy and applicable consumer rules; never count a nonrefundable amount you are not entitled to keep.</p>
<p><strong>Event spend.</strong> A borrowed location may cost less than a studio; a paid assistant or seasonal promotion may cost more. Put only the event's real charges in this event base. If you reuse a backdrop on several shoots, use a sensible cost allocation rather than charging its full purchase to the first event and nothing to later ones.</p>
<p><strong>Time-slot capacity.</strong> A 15-minute shoot plus 15-minute buffer yields eight slots in four hours. If clients need wardrobe changes or the setup varies, 30-minute slots may be unrealistic. A longer slot reduces available capacity, even if the per-slot selling price stays the same. Check what you can deliver without rushing people or breaking the gallery deadline.</p>
<p><strong>Upsells.</strong> An additional print or image may add contribution, but only if the buyer orders it and you include its production, processing, shipping and editing cost. Price the base event to stand on its own; treat uncertain upsells as upside, not the way the base package breaks even.</p>
<h2 id="set-up-the-next-event-from-your-own-records">Set up the next event from your own records</h2>
<p>Before opening bookings, write a one-page offer: date, location, slot length, included people and images, turnaround time, reschedule terms, payment schedule and any extra-image price. Estimate promotion, space and assistant expense, then reserve owner hours for setup and the whole slot block. Use your own last event's editing and support time where available; otherwise label each time as an assumption and review it afterwards.</p>
<p>Track inquiry source, booked slots, paid slots, completed slots, edits delivered and final owner-hours separately. If six slots fill immediately and two stay empty, the data may guide your next date or advertising spend. If all eight book but every gallery needs another hour, it points to deliverables or retouching, not demand. Our <a href="/blogs/how-many-photography-clients-month/">photography clients-per-month guide</a> handles monthly booking goals; this event sheet tests one Saturday's economics.</p>
<h2 id="how-to-run-your-own-monthly-numbers">How to run your own monthly numbers</h2>
<p>To see whether the event contributes enough toward monthly insurance, software, equipment costs and planned owner salary, use the <a href="https://mybreakeven.com/calculators/photography-business-break-even-calculator/">photography business break-even calculator</a>. Enter a representative completed booking and realistic monthly capacity; keep event-only space and ad costs in the scenario without also counting them as a second fixed bill. The tool accepts other currencies as well as the USD used here.</p>
<h2 id="common-mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
<p><strong>Counting eight 15-minute shoots as two hours of work.</strong> In this example, the on-site block alone is four hours, before setup, editing and delivery.</p>
<p><strong>Calling unpaid owner hours profit.</strong> Decide whether your reported surplus is before or after paying the person who shoots and edits.</p>
<p><strong>Treating available slots as sales.</strong> Only paid, retained bookings belong in the revenue line. Model cancellations under the real policy.</p>
<p><strong>Making each mini session a custom shoot.</strong> Additional backgrounds, locations and revisions consume the time savings on which the mini format relies.</p>
<p><strong>Forgetting event acquisition cost.</strong> Paid promotion may be a fixed event cost even if no one books; include it before calculating the minimum slots.</p>
<p><strong>Assuming an upsell rescues the base price.</strong> It might not happen, and fulfillment still costs time and money.</p>
<h2 id="faqs">FAQs</h2>
<h3 id="how-many-mini-sessions-should-i-book-to-break-even">How many mini sessions should I book to break even?</h3>
<p>Divide the cost of staging the event by the contribution left from one paid booking after its variable fee and delivery time. In the illustrative case, $630 ÷ $177.50 is 3.55, so four paid slots cover modeled costs.</p>
<h3 id="is-a-15-minute-session-only-15-minutes-of-photographer-time">Is a 15-minute session only 15 minutes of photographer time?</h3>
<p>No. You need appointment buffers, setup, selection, editing, delivery and messages. Measure those hours before choosing a per-slot price.</p>
<h3 id="should-editing-be-included-in-the-package">Should editing be included in the package?</h3>
<p>State exactly how many edited files and what level of retouching are included, then budget the required time. Charge separately or set a limit for additional requests so the event remains deliverable.</p>
<h3 id="what-if-a-customer-does-not-show-up">What if a customer does not show up?</h3>
<p>Use your actual booking and payment terms to decide what revenue remains; the reserved studio and owner time may still cost you money. Track no-shows apart from unsold slots when reviewing the event.</p>
<h3 id="should-i-count-studio-rent-if-i-own-my-space">Should I count studio rent if I own my space?</h3>
<p>Include an appropriate share of ongoing occupancy cost in your business-wide model. If the space has a genuine extra event-specific cost, record that separately rather than counting the same rent twice.</p>
<h3 id="can-i-price-the-base-package-assuming-print-upgrades">Can I price the base package assuming print upgrades?</h3>
<p>It is safer to check whether the basic paid slot works on its own. Run an optional second scenario using measured print attachment and print-production cost; do not treat unbooked extras as certain sales.</p>
<h2 id="takeaways">Takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>Price the entire short-session workflow, including edits, messages and paid owner time.</li>
<li>Separate the $630 event base from the $47.50 of extra cost per booked client in the example.</li>
<li>Test three, four, six and eight paid slots; the same event can lose money or earn a surplus.</li>
<li>Use actual editing times and cancellations to improve the next date and offer.</li>
</ul>`,faq:[{q:`How many mini sessions should I book to break even?`,a:`Divide the cost of staging the event by the contribution left from one paid booking after its variable fee and delivery time. In the illustrative case, $630 ÷ $177.50 is 3.55, so four paid slots cover modeled costs.`},{q:`Is a 15-minute session only 15 minutes of photographer time?`,a:`No. You need appointment buffers, setup, selection, editing, delivery and messages. Measure those hours before choosing a per-slot price.`},{q:`Should editing be included in the package?`,a:`State exactly how many edited files and what level of retouching are included, then budget the required time. Charge separately or set a limit for additional requests so the event remains deliverable.`},{q:`What if a customer does not show up?`,a:`Use your actual booking and payment terms to decide what revenue remains; the reserved studio and owner time may still cost you money. Track no-shows apart from unsold slots when reviewing the event.`},{q:`Should I count studio rent if I own my space?`,a:`Include an appropriate share of ongoing occupancy cost in your business-wide model. If the space has a genuine extra event-specific cost, record that separately rather than counting the same rent twice.`},{q:`Can I price the base package assuming print upgrades?`,a:`It is safer to check whether the basic paid slot works on its own. Run an optional second scenario using measured print attachment and print-production cost; do not treat unbooked extras as certain sales.`}]},{slug:`fleet-detailing-contract-pricing`,tag:`Mobile Detailing`,name:`Mobile Detailing`,cluster:`pricing`,title:`How to Price a Fleet Detailing Contract`,seoTitle:`Fleet Detailing Contract Pricing: Worked Quote`,description:`Price a fleet detailing contract from vehicles, visit frequency, technician hours, supplies and route costs. Check a worked quote and fifth-visit risk.`,metaDescription:`Price a fleet detailing contract from vehicles, visit frequency, technician hours, supplies and route costs. Check a worked quote and fifth-visit risk.`,image:`/images/blog/fleet-detailing-contract-pricing-editorial.webp`,alt:`Mobile detailer reviews a checklist beside a row of unbranded work vans`,imageCaption:`Illustrative editorial image; quoted prices below are fictional planning inputs.`,published:`2026-09-29`,modified:`2026-09-29`,opening:`Twelve vans at one site can look like the perfect route: no driving between customers and a repeat booking on the calendar. But a contract that says “clean the fleet weekly” can quietly require a fifth visit, extra interior work or more vehicle types than you priced. Quote the number of vehicles, the exact number of visits and the cleaning scope before turning a per-van rate into a monthly promise.`,unit:`vehicles`,singular:`vehicle`,calculatorSlug:`mobile-detailing-break-even-calculator`,tags:[`Mobile Detailing`,`Fleet Contracts`,`Pricing`],features:[`A checked worked example`,`Clearly stated assumptions`,`Practical capacity and risk checks`],html:`<p>Twelve vans at one site can look like the perfect route: no driving between customers and a repeat booking on the calendar. But a contract that says “clean the fleet weekly” can quietly require a fifth visit, extra interior work or more vehicle types than you priced. Quote the <strong>number of vehicles, the exact number of visits and the cleaning scope</strong> before turning a per-van rate into a monthly promise.</p>
<p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Estimate paid technician-hours per vehicle and per site visit. Add supplies, travel, equipment use, account administration and a consistent share of overhead, then test the price against the visits and vehicle count named in the contract. In the illustrative example below, 12 vans cleaned on four visits cost <strong>$2,868</strong> to service and support; billing $80 per vehicle-visit yields <strong>$3,840</strong> and a modeled <strong>$972 surplus</strong>. A fifth visit included at no extra charge cuts that surplus to $470.</p>
<p>This guide prices a <strong>recurring group of vehicles at one account</strong>, not a one-off retail detail or an individual maintenance plan. See our <a href="/blogs/mobile-detailing-maintenance-plan-pricing/">mobile detailing maintenance-plan pricing guide</a> for a recurring offer to individual customers.</p>
<h2 id="define-the-fleet-service-before-calculating-a-price">Define the fleet service before calculating a price</h2>
<p>Walk the lot and count the vehicles by class. A small sedan, cargo van and heavily used service truck can demand different work. Record interior condition, exterior wash method, cab access, equipment storage, water and power access, allowed work hours and how keys will be managed. Ask whether a vehicle may be unavailable during a planned visit and whether the client expects you to return for it. State which surfaces receive a basic clean and which stains, odor treatments, paint corrections or damage repairs are separate work.</p>
<p>Choose a billing unit that the client can understand: <strong>one vehicle cleaned on one scheduled visit</strong>. In this example the customer has 12 similar vans and buys exactly <strong>four scheduled site visits per billing month</strong>. That creates 12 × 4 = <strong>48 vehicle-visits</strong>. A calendar month with a fifth weekly service is <em>not</em> included by that wording; define its price and approval process in the proposal. If the client actually needs every week of the year, you can instead price 52 annual visits divided into 12 equal payments, but the annual service count must appear in the agreement.</p>
<p>The numbers below are <strong>fictional USD assumptions</strong> for a worked calculation. They are not typical US detailing rates or a quote for any actual fleet.</p>
<div class="article-table-scroll"><table>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Cost component</th>
<th>Four-visit monthly assumption</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Cost</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Technician vehicle work</td>
<td>48 vehicles × 0.75 paid hour × $32</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$1,152</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Technician setup and closeout</td>
<td>4 visits × 1.5 paid hours × $32</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$192</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Supplies and consumables</td>
<td>48 vehicle-visits × $8</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$384</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Account travel</td>
<td>4 visits × $50</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$200</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Equipment use and site handling</td>
<td>4 visits × $20</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$80</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Account supervision and administration</td>
<td>Monthly allowance</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$260</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Share of company overhead</td>
<td>Monthly allocation</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$600</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td><strong>Total modeled cost</strong></td>
<td></td>
<td style="text-align: right;"><strong>$2,868</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Technician labor is <strong>42 paid hours</strong>, not 42 hours on the wall clock if two people work at once: 48 × 0.75 = 36 vehicle-hours, plus 4 × 1.5 = 6 setup-hours. At $32 fully loaded cost per paid technician-hour, labor is <strong>$1,344</strong>. Use your own payroll and paid travel practice in place of $32.</p>
<p>At $80 per vehicle-visit, revenue is 48 × $80 = <strong>$3,840</strong>. Modeled surplus after all the listed costs is $3,840 − $2,868 = <strong>$972</strong>, or 25.31% of client revenue. The $600 is an allocated share of shared monthly overhead. It can make a contract-level full-cost estimate useful, but when you later run a business-wide break-even model, count that shared overhead in fixed costs <strong>only once</strong>.</p>
<h2 id="check-the-schedule-before-offering-a-volume-discount">Check the schedule before offering a volume discount</h2>
<p>Fleet work is attractive when the route is dense and each vehicle fits the expected scope. It is less attractive when drivers move keys late, vehicles arrive muddy after a job or the team must wait for access. The estimate uses 45 paid minutes per van plus 1.5 paid setup/closeout hours per visit. One visit therefore takes <strong>10.5 paid technician-hours</strong> (12 × 0.75 + 1.5). If two technicians are available, that does not automatically mean 5.25 hours on site: task handoffs, water access and parking can add elapsed time.</p>
<p>Promise a realistic service window. Record how many vehicles can be out of service simultaneously and whether your crew can move them. If client employees must hand over keys, agree on a ready-by time and on what happens to a vehicle not presented. Travel and equipment setup are paid once per visit in the base case; if the client requests a return visit for a missed van, that is a new trip, not simply another $8 of supplies.</p>
<p>To compare a discounted fleet rate with single-vehicle work, use <strong>contribution</strong>, not the retail sticker price. The 48 vehicle-visits each require $24 in technician vehicle time (0.75 × $32) and $8 supplies. At an $80 price, they leave <strong>$48 per vehicle-visit</strong> toward setup, travel, equipment, account administration and overhead. The shared monthly costs beyond per-vehicle labor and supplies total $2,868 − (48 × $32) = <strong>$1,332</strong>. A 48-visit contract contributes 48 × $48 = $2,304; after $1,332, the same $972 surplus remains.</p>
<p>This form makes a lower vehicle count visible. On unchanged four-visit trips, the modeled break-even number of vehicle-visits is $1,332 ÷ $48 = 27.75, or <strong>28 completed vehicle-visits</strong>. That is seven vans on each of four visits. It is a mathematical threshold under the stated mix, not a reason to accept any seven-vehicle account: available parking, condition and access may change the costs.</p>
<h2 id="two-changes-that-can-consume-the-margin">Two changes that can consume the margin</h2>
<p><strong>A fifth visit with no additional bill.</strong> Another 12 vehicle cleans and one site setup cost 12 × 0.75 × $32 = $288 of vehicle labor, 1.5 × $32 = $48 setup labor, 12 × $8 = $96 supplies, $50 travel and $20 equipment. Extra cost is <strong>$502</strong>. If the price stays $3,840, total cost rises to $3,370 and surplus falls to <strong>$470</strong>, a 12.24% margin. A clear four-visit term avoids ambiguity. If the customer buys the fifth visit at the same $80 per vehicle, additional revenue is $960 and the five-visit modeled surplus becomes <strong>$1,430</strong> on $4,800 revenue.</p>
<p><strong>Fewer vehicles, same trips.</strong> If only nine vans are presented on each of four visits, there are 36 vehicle-visits. Per-vehicle labor and supplies cost 36 × $32 = $1,152. Add the same $1,332 of visit and monthly costs: total <strong>$2,484</strong>. Billing only 36 × $80 = $2,880 leaves <strong>$396</strong>, a 13.75% margin. To protect the economics, agree a minimum monthly account charge, a minimum count, or a separate trip/setup fee. Make the rule visible in the quote rather than surprising the customer later.</p>
<div class="article-table-scroll"><table>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Scenario</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Billable vehicle-visits</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Client price</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Modeled cost</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">Surplus</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Four visits, 12 vans each</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">48</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$3,840</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$2,868</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$972</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Five visits, fifth included free</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">60</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$3,840</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$3,370</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$470</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Four visits, nine vans each</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">36</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$2,880</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$2,484</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">$396</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The $1,332 shared/visit cost assumes four trips in the reduced-count case. For the fifth-visit case, the extra setup, travel and equipment are added explicitly, so do not reuse $1,332 unchanged with 60 vehicle-visits.</p>
<h2 id="turn-the-calculation-into-a-usable-proposal">Turn the calculation into a usable proposal</h2>
<p>Name the account location, vehicle types, included interior and exterior tasks, maximum time or condition assumptions, the four dates or scheduling method, service window, client access duties, handling for missed vehicles and your change-order price for extra visits, oversize units or heavy cleanup. Say who supplies water/power, who handles waste and how completion is signed off. Set billing and payment terms so payroll can be paid before the invoice clears.</p>
<p>Vehicle washing can create wastewater; ask how the site handles it before quoting a method. The US EPA's <a href="https://www.epa.gov/system/files/documents/2023-01/bmp-vehicle-maintenance-and-washing.pdf">vehicle maintenance and washing best-practice document</a> explains the stormwater concern. Confirm the local site rules and applicable requirements rather than assuming a standard parking-lot wash is appropriate everywhere. If the service must use additional containment or an approved facility, price that method and travel into this specific account.</p>
<p>Save actual arrival times, vehicles cleaned, paid technician-hours, materials and return trips after the first few visits. A contract price can be corrected at renewal if your original quote was wrong; an extra or changed service should be handled through the agreed approval process. Our <a href="/blogs/mobile-detailing-break-even/">mobile detailing break-even guide</a> covers whole-business monthly job capacity. The <a href="/blogs/car-detailing-prices-list/">car-detailing prices guide</a> helps distinguish a basic recurring clean from a deeper retail service.</p>
<h2 id="how-to-run-your-own-monthly-break-even-check">How to run your own monthly break-even check</h2>
<p>Use the <a href="https://mybreakeven.com/calculators/mobile-detailing-break-even-calculator/">mobile detailing break-even calculator</a> with a representative completed vehicle service or account visit, its job-level variable cost, company-wide monthly fixed expenses and owner pay. Keep the account's $600 allocated overhead out of variable cost if those same shared expenses are already entered as monthly fixed costs. The calculator accepts currencies other than USD; the example here is simply in dollars.</p>
<h2 id="common-mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
<p><strong>Calling four visits “weekly forever.”</strong> Four times per month and every seven days all year are different service promises.</p>
<p><strong>Pricing only the time touching vehicles.</strong> Paid setup, closeout, waiting and key handling may occupy the crew even when no vehicle is being cleaned.</p>
<p><strong>Offering a volume discount without a vehicle minimum.</strong> The same trip and setup cost can be spread over 12 vans or only nine.</p>
<p><strong>Treating every van as identical.</strong> Condition, size and included interior work need defined classes or approved extras.</p>
<p><strong>Ignoring site wastewater handling.</strong> A lower price based on an unsuitable wash method is not a useful quote.</p>
<p><strong>Counting allocated overhead twice.</strong> Use it in the full-cost contract view or the monthly company break-even input, with consistent treatment.</p>
<h2 id="faqs">FAQs</h2>
<h3 id="should-i-quote-per-vehicle-or-per-month">Should I quote per vehicle or per month?</h3>
<p>You can show both. Calculate a cost per vehicle-visit internally, then state the exact vehicle count and scheduled visits behind the monthly price. Include a minimum or approved extra-visit price where appropriate.</p>
<h3 id="what-if-the-fleet-has-different-types-of-vehicles">What if the fleet has different types of vehicles?</h3>
<p>Group them by actual service time and scope, then price each class or set a clearly defined mix assumption. A cargo truck with a dirty cab should not silently receive the same time allowance as a small van.</p>
<h3 id="how-do-i-handle-a-fifth-weekly-visit-in-a-long-month">How do I handle a fifth weekly visit in a long month?</h3>
<p>Define whether the contract includes four visits per billing month or a weekly schedule throughout the year. Price the fifth visit separately or build the full annual visit count into equal monthly payments.</p>
<h3 id="do-i-bill-for-a-van-that-was-not-available">Do I bill for a van that was not available?</h3>
<p>Use the agreed minimum, readiness and rescheduling terms. If only completed vehicles are billed, test the reduced count while keeping unavoidable travel and setup costs in the model.</p>
<h3 id="what-is-the-break-even-vehicle-count-in-the-example">What is the break-even vehicle count in the example?</h3>
<p>With unchanged four visits, each completed vehicle-visit contributes $48 after per-vehicle labor and supplies. The $1,332 visit/monthly base divided by $48 is 27.75, so 28 completed vehicle-visits, equivalent to seven vans across four visits.</p>
<h3 id="is-the-972-surplus-the-owners-take-home-pay">Is the $972 surplus the owner's take-home pay?</h3>
<p>Only if your cost model already includes the owner's planned pay and all other relevant company costs. If the owner is doing unpaid work in this model, add realistic compensation before calling the remainder a true operating surplus.</p>
<h2 id="takeaways">Takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>Define four visits, 12 vans and the service scope before quoting a per-vehicle price.</li>
<li>Model technician-hours, setup, supplies, travel and account overhead separately.</li>
<li>Test a fifth visit and reduced vehicle count; both change the economics materially.</li>
<li>Document minimums, extras, site access and wastewater method in the agreement.</li>
</ul>`,faq:[{q:`Should I quote per vehicle or per month?`,a:`You can show both. Calculate a cost per vehicle-visit internally, then state the exact vehicle count and scheduled visits behind the monthly price. Include a minimum or approved extra-visit price where appropriate.`},{q:`What if the fleet has different types of vehicles?`,a:`Group them by actual service time and scope, then price each class or set a clearly defined mix assumption. A cargo truck with a dirty cab should not silently receive the same time allowance as a small van.`},{q:`How do I handle a fifth weekly visit in a long month?`,a:`Define whether the contract includes four visits per billing month or a weekly schedule throughout the year. Price the fifth visit separately or build the full annual visit count into equal monthly payments.`},{q:`Do I bill for a van that was not available?`,a:`Use the agreed minimum, readiness and rescheduling terms. If only completed vehicles are billed, test the reduced count while keeping unavoidable travel and setup costs in the model.`},{q:`What is the break-even vehicle count in the example?`,a:`With unchanged four visits, each completed vehicle-visit contributes $48 after per-vehicle labor and supplies. The $1,332 visit/monthly base divided by $48 is 27.75, so 28 completed vehicle-visits, equivalent to seven vans across four visits.`},{q:`Is the $972 surplus the owner's take-home pay?`,a:`Only if your cost model already includes the owner's planned pay and all other relevant company costs. If the owner is doing unpaid work in this model, add realistic compensation before calling the remainder a true operating surplus.`}]}];export{e as september29Posts};